#pragma once

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Gas giant banding and flow. Three baked volumes, one Custom node.
//
// WHY GAS GIANTS BAND, since the whole design follows from it.
//
// Rapid rotation makes the flow quasi-2D. Two-dimensional turbulence cascades
// energy UPWARD in scale rather than downward, so structure grows instead of
// dissipating. On a sphere the Coriolis parameter varies with latitude, which
// makes that cascade anisotropic: it feeds east-west modes preferentially,
// because north-south motion radiates Rossby waves and east-west motion does
// not. The cascade arrests at the Rhines scale, into zonal jets.
//
// The forcing is isotropic. The banding is anisotropic STRAINING of isotropic
// structure. So isotropic noise, differentially rotated, is not an
// approximation of the process -- it is the process.
//
// THE THREE OPERATIONS, deliberately separated.
//
//   Structure   Filaments and whorls. Comes free from sampling the field at a
//               differentially rotated position. Static in time, so its
//               magnitude is unbounded and costs nothing.
//
//   Transport   Bands orbiting at their own rates. The SAME rotation, with
//               time in it. Rigid per latitude, so exact and good forever.
//
//   Evolution   Things changing rather than merely moving. A small drift of
//               the lookup, bounded.
//
// Structure and transport being one operation is the point. SpinUpTime is
// literally how long the planet has been turning before frame zero: start at
// t = 40 rather than t = 0 and the field opens already wound into filaments.
//
// WHY ROTATION AND NOT DISPLACEMENT.
//
// No offset added to a coordinate produces orbiting; it produces scrolling
// across an axis, and on a sphere it also drags latitude. Displacing along the
// east vector by c and renormalizing moves along a CHORD, so the length becomes
// sqrt(1 + c*c) and the latitude creeps equatorward as c grows.
//
// Rotation about Z preserves P.z and |P| identically. Every latitude ring maps
// onto itself, the Jacobian determinant is exactly 1, and there is no
// accumulation error to reset. A latitude-only pattern is invariant under it,
// which is the precise statement that zonal flow cannot disturb its own bands.
//
// WHY GRADIENT AND NOT CURL, for both flow textures.
//
// Curl is divergence-free in 3D, but the 3D divergence includes radial terms
// that projecting onto the tangent plane discards, so a projected curl field is
// NOT divergence-free on the sphere. cross(N, grad psi) is, exactly, for any
// scalar psi. Gradient is also half the bake: six evaluations against twelve,
// one noise instance against three.
//
// WHAT THE CROSSFADE IS FOR.
//
// Not drift; the rotation is exact. Winding eventually drives filaments below
// texel width, and the crossfade is what bounds that.
//
// Its visible cost scales with Period/SpinUpTime: across one cycle the total
// shear goes from SpinUp to SpinUp + Period, so with SpinUp large that is a few
// percent and the reset is nearly invisible. More filamentation and a less
// visible reset are the same knob turned the same way.
//
// The weight of a phase must fall to ZERO at the instant that phase resets.
// That is the only thing hiding the discontinuity, and inverting it makes the
// reset play at full opacity as a hard edge sweeping the surface.
//
// THE THREE TEXTURES.
//
//   CoarseFlow   Gradient + ScalarPotential alpha, RGBA16F. RGB is grad psi,
//                A is psi itself, read as pressure -- cross(N, grad psi) is the
//                geostrophic relation, so the circulation around a high or low
//                is not decorated on afterwards, it falls out of the same
//                derivative. Drives the primary warp. Crossfaded.
//
//   DetailFlow   Gradient + INDEPENDENT alpha, RGBA16F. RGB is a finer grad
//                psi for the second warp octave; A is a free channel, best
//                spent on Worley F2-F1, whose cell walls give the filament
//                network directly. The C0 warning on F2-F1 does not apply here
//                because this channel is read, never differentiated.
//                Crossfaded.
//
//   PackedDetail Four independent scalars, BGRA8. Not crossfaded -- see
//                GG_GasGiant. This is where the 4x storage saving belongs,
//                since all four channels are unipolar.
//
// ALL SAMPLERS MUST BE WRAP ON ALL THREE AXES.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Steps used to integrate the coarse warp.
 *
 *  1 is a single straight displacement, which is what a domain warp normally is
 *  and what this had before. It cannot produce roll-up: material moves once
 *  along the local flow direction and stops, so the SHAPE of the streamline
 *  never reaches the output no matter what the field contains.
 *
 *  2 or more traces the streamline instead. Total displacement is unchanged --
 *  each step takes 1/N of it -- but the path bends as it goes, so material
 *  actually winds around a vortex. This is what makes Swirl.x mean
 *  anything at all, and what lets a filament curl back into a swirl rather than
 *  running off a band forever.
 *
 *  Costs one fetch per step per phase. 2 is usually enough; 3 curls harder. */
#ifndef GG_WARP_ITERATIONS
#define GG_WARP_ITERATIONS 4
#endif

/** Collapse both crossfade phases into one. A DIAGNOSTIC, not a shipping mode:
 *  shear then accumulates without bound and will eventually alias. Set it to
 *  find out whether an artifact is the crossfade or the field, because two
 *  phases far enough apart in shear are sampling unrelated states and blending
 *  them produces hooks and doubled contours that look nothing like either. */
#ifndef GG_SINGLE_PHASE
#define GG_SINGLE_PHASE 0
#endif

/** Replace the returned cloud top with the step count, normalized against
 *  InMaxSteps, for a cost heatmap. */
#ifndef GG_DEBUG_STEPS
#define GG_DEBUG_STEPS 0
#endif

/** Enable the second warp octave and its ridge channel. Costs one extra fetch
 *  PER PHASE, so two in total. Worth it on a surface; the first thing to switch
 *  off if this construction moves into a raymarch loop. */
#ifndef GG_DETAIL_OCTAVE
#define GG_DETAIL_OCTAVE 1
#endif

/** Include the packed detail layer. One extra fetch PER PHASE. */
#ifndef GG_PACKED_DETAIL
#define GG_PACKED_DETAIL 1
#endif

/** Replace the band output with the raw crossfade weight, for checking that the
 *  reset is invisible. Should be smooth low-frequency mush with no correlation
 *  to the bands or the flow; any hard contour is a reset showing through. */
#ifndef GG_DEBUG_CROSSFADE
#define GG_DEBUG_CROSSFADE 0
#endif

/** Rotation about the planet's axis. The only transport primitive used here.
 *
 *  Exact for any angle, so it never needs to be small and never needs a reset.
 *  Preserves P.z, which is what leaves the bands undisturbed. */
float3 GG_RotateZ(float3 P, float Angle)
{
    float SinA, CosA;
    sincos(Angle, SinA, CosA);

    return float3(
		P.x * CosA - P.y * SinA,
		P.x * SinA + P.y * CosA,
		P.z);
}

/** The raw alternating shape, before plateau shaping. Roughly [-1.45, 1.45].
 *
 *  EVEN in S, so mirrored latitudes rotate the same way. An odd form makes the
 *  hemispheres counter-rotate, which puts a shear seam along the equator and is
 *  not what any real gas giant does.
 *
 *  Written in sin(latitude) rather than latitude so jets come out equal AREA
 *  rather than equal angle, which stops every band crowding into the poles.
 *
 *  The second cosine is at an incommensurate frequency so jet spacing stops
 *  looking regular. Both terms are pure cosines with no phase offset, which is
 *  what keeps the sum even and also smooth across the equator -- a phase offset
 *  or an abs() would put a kink at S = 0.
 *
 *  Asymmetry adds a small ODD term back. Perfect mirror symmetry is its own
 *  artifact: the eye picks it up immediately and it reads as a decal rather
 *  than a planet. */
float GG_JetRaw(float S, float BandCount, float Asymmetry)
{
    const float K = BandCount * PI;

    float Shape = cos(K * S);
    Shape += 0.45f * cos(K * 1.7f * S);
    Shape += Asymmetry * sin(K * 0.6f * S);

    return Shape;
}

/** d(JetRaw)/dS in closed form. Term-for-term the derivative of GG_JetRaw,
 *  scaled the same way, so the two cannot drift apart silently.
 *
 *  This is the local shear rate per unit time, and it is what tells the relief
 *  how thin a region has been stretched. */
float GG_JetRawSlope(float S, float BandCount, float Asymmetry)
{
    const float K = BandCount * PI;

    float D = -K * sin(K * S);
    D += -0.45f * K * 1.7f * sin(K * 1.7f * S);
    D += Asymmetry * K * 0.6f * cos(K * 0.6f * S);

    return D * 0.6897f;
}

/** Plateau shaping, and the boundary mask that falls out of it.
 *
 *  THIS IS WHY BANDS HAVE A SOLID MIDDLE.
 *
 *  A sum of cosines is curving everywhere and is flat nowhere, so a band built
 *  from one has no interior -- only a rising edge that immediately becomes a
 *  falling edge. Jupiter is the opposite: wide flat zones and belts with narrow
 *  transitions between them. Pushing the raw shape through a smoothstep of
 *  controllable width produces genuine plateaus, and the width is the single
 *  control over how much of a band is middle versus edge.
 *
 *  THIS IS ALSO WHY TURBULENCE BELONGS AT THE EDGES.
 *
 *  Shaping the RATE this way, not just the brightness, means dRate/dS falls to
 *  near zero inside a plateau and spikes at the crossing. Shear concentrates at
 *  band boundaries by construction rather than by a separate mask, which is
 *  where a real gas giant's curls and billows are and where a uniform-shear
 *  model puts them everywhere equally.
 *
 *  The consequence worth planning around: SpinUpTime now acts almost entirely
 *  on the boundaries. Winding hard enough to stretch features horizontal no
 *  longer shreds the zone interiors into micro-banding, because the interiors
 *  are barely being wound at all. Flatness and SpinUpTime stop fighting.
 *
 *  Shaping is MONOTONE, so it moves no zero crossings. Bands and jets stay
 *  registered with each other however hard this is driven.
 *
 *  BandShape.x  Flatness. 0 leaves the raw curve, 1 is nearly a square wave.
 *  BandShape.z  Relief thinning; see GG_EvaluatePhase.
 *  BandShape.y  Width bias. Offsets the crossing so zones and belts need not
 *               be the same width, which on Jupiter they are not -- belts are
 *               the narrower of the two.
 *
 *  OutBoundary is 1 at a transition and 0 in a plateau. It is 4t(1-t) on the
 *  smoothstep's own parameter, so it costs a multiply and tracks the shaping
 *  exactly rather than approximating it. */
void GG_JetShaped(
	float S,
	float BandCount,
	float Asymmetry,
	float3 BandShape,
	out float OutShape,
	out float OutBoundary,
	out float OutRaw)
{
	// 1/1.45, normalizing the raw range to roughly [-1,1] so the width
	// parameters mean the same thing regardless of Asymmetry.
    const float Raw = GG_JetRaw(S, BandCount, Asymmetry) * 0.6897f - BandShape.y;

    const float Width = max(1.0f - BandShape.x, 0.02f);

    const float T = saturate((Raw + Width) / (2.0f * Width));

    OutShape = 2.0f * (T * T * (3.0f - 2.0f * T)) - 1.0f;
    OutBoundary = 4.0f * T * (1.0f - T);

	// Unshaped, and clamped only. The rate uses THIS rather than OutShape,
	// because a plateau has zero slope and dRate/dS is the entire source of
	// shear -- shaping the rate flattens the winding out of every band interior
	// and leaves ShearFalloff scaling something that is already zero there.
	//
	// Registration survives the split: both are monotone functions of the same
	// raw value, so they share every zero crossing however differently they are
	// shaped. Turbulence stays concentrated at the boundaries through
	// OutBoundary, which is a mask rather than a slope and does not need the
	// rate flattened to work.
    OutRaw = clamp(Raw, -1.0f, 1.0f);
}

/** Angular rate. Radians per unit time.
 *
 *  Rate, not linear speed. A linear speed would have to be divided by cos(lat)
 *  to become an angle, which is singular at the poles for no benefit.
 *
 *  EquatorialBoost widens and strengthens the jet at S = 0. The base shape
 *  already puts a prograde jet there, since cos(0) is 1 -- that comes free with
 *  evenness and is correct for Jupiter -- so this shapes an existing jet rather
 *  than inventing one.
 *
 *  It is added AFTER the plateau shaping, deliberately. Shaping it would flatten
 *  the equatorial jet into just another plateau and lose the one profile feature
 *  that is genuinely a peak rather than a step.
 *
 *  What makes STRUCTURE is dRate/dS, not Rate. A constant added to every
 *  latitude is a rigid spin and produces no shear at all; only the differences
 *  between latitudes wind anything. That is why RigidRate is pulled out of the
 *  crossfade downstream, and why setting it near the profile's PEAK rather than
 *  its mean will flatten the shear there and make SpinUpTime appear to stop
 *  working. */
float GG_ZonalRate(
	float S,
	float BandCount,
	float JetStrength,
	float EquatorialBoost,
	float Asymmetry,
	float3 BandShape)
{
    float Shape, Boundary, Raw;
    GG_JetShaped(S, BandCount, Asymmetry, BandShape, Shape, Boundary, Raw);

    const float Boost = EquatorialBoost * exp(-S * S * 12.0f);

    return JetStrength * (Raw + Boost);
}

/** Band brightness, derived from the same shape as the jets.
 *
 *  Bands mark jets -- zones and belts are the visible signature of the shear
 *  between them -- so shaping the two with independent functions that merely
 *  share a frequency lets them drift out of phase, and then the bands sit in
 *  the wrong place relative to the flow supposedly making them.
 *
 *  Takes an already-warped coordinate, so meander and jitter survive.
 *
 *  BandSharpness now only adds contrast on top of the plateau. With Flatness
 *  doing the flattening it can stay near 1; driving it hard clips the shaped
 *  value and terraces the height field. */
float GG_BandProfile(
	float BandCoord,
	float BandCount,
	float Asymmetry,
	float3 BandShape,
	float BandSharpness)
{
    float Shape, Boundary, Raw;
    GG_JetShaped(BandCoord, BandCount, Asymmetry, BandShape, Shape, Boundary, Raw);

    return saturate(0.5f + 0.5f * Shape * BandSharpness);
}

/** Low-frequency static per-point phase offset for the crossfade.
 *
 *  Must be constant in time for a given surface point, so it is a function of
 *  the UNSHEARED direction. Analytic rather than a fetch because it only needs
 *  to be decorrelated, not to look like anything.
 *
 *  Deliberately low frequency. High-frequency jitter makes neighbouring texels
 *  reset at very different times, which reads as boiling. */
float GG_PhaseJitter(float3 N)
{
    const float A = sin(dot(N, float3(3.7f, 1.9f, 2.3f)));
    const float B = sin(dot(N, float3(-1.7f, 3.1f, 4.3f)));

    return 0.5f + 0.5f * A * B;
}

/** Perlin dilated by inverted Worley -- the standard cloud-shape combine.
 *
 *  Perlin alone is smooth undulation with no sense of a cloud EDGE. Worley F1
 *  inverted is billowy blobs with no interior variation. Remapping the first by
 *  the second keeps Perlin's detail while giving it Worley's rounded boundary,
 *  and that rounded boundary is what reads as fluffiness.
 *
 *  This has to happen in the shader rather than the bake because the baker's
 *  channels are independent by construction -- there is no cross-channel
 *  operation in the recipe model, and adding one would compromise the thing
 *  that makes the probe, grouping and brick bounds work. Two channels out, one
 *  combine in, no fetch cost.
 *
 *  Worley here is the raw F1, which is LOW at cell centres, so the remap floor
 *  uses it directly rather than inverted. */
float GG_PerlinWorley(float Perlin, float WorleyF1)
{
    const float Low = -WorleyF1;

    return saturate((Perlin - Low) / max(1.0f - Low, 1e-3f));
}

/** One crossfade phase. Everything sheared lives here and it runs twice.
 *
 *  PER-LAYER SHEAR, which is the reason ShearFalloff exists.
 *
 *  Shear turns a feature of size L into one L(1+g) long and L/(1+g) wide. Once
 *  that narrow dimension falls below a texel the feature aliases rather than
 *  filaments. Coarse features survive a large g and become the long streaks
 *  that make the look; fine features hit the texel limit at a fraction of the
 *  same g and dissolve into grain. Shearing every layer by the same amount is
 *  therefore wrong, and it is wrong in a way that gets worse the more detail
 *  you add.
 *
 *  It is also wrong physically. Eddy turnover time scales with eddy size, so a
 *  small eddy is regenerated long before it accumulates much strain. Only the
 *  large structures live long enough to be wound. ShearFalloff is that
 *  lifetime ratio, and it should fall roughly as the inverse of each layer's
 *  frequency.
 *
 *  Each layer still inherits the DISPLACEMENT of the layers above it, so the
 *  fine structure sits on the coarse filaments. Only the accumulated strain
 *  differs. Position and strain history are separate things and this is the
 *  one place that distinction earns its keep.
 *
 *  Scales        float4(Coarse, Detail, Packed, PackedWarpInherit)
 *  Warps         float4(CoarseWarp, DetailWarp, BandJitter, TurbulenceFloor)
 *  Detail        float4(Ridge, Mottle, Wisp, EdgeBias)
 *  ShearFalloff  float4(Coarse, Detail, Packed, DetailWarpInherit)
 *
 *  Returns float4(Band, Pressure, Composition, StormCells). */
float4 GG_EvaluatePhase(
	Texture3D CoarseFlow,
	SamplerState CoarseSampler,
	Texture3D DetailFlow,
	SamplerState DetailSampler,
	Texture3D PackedDetail,
	SamplerState PackedSampler,
	float3 P,
	float3 N,
	float S,
	float ShearTime,
	float DriftTime,
	float RigidRate,
	float3 Drift,
	float4 Scales,
	float4 Warps,
	float4 Detail,
	float4 ShearFalloff,
	float BandCount,
	float JetStrength,
	float EquatorialBoost,
	float Asymmetry,
	float3 BandShape,
	float BandSharpness,
	float2 Swirl)
{
	// A rotation shared by every latitude produces no shear at all, so it does
	// not wind anything and has no business inside the crossfade. Pulled out
	// here, both phases share it exactly and the only mismatch left to blend is
	// the residual differential rotation, which is what the crossfade is for.
	//
	// It is also the term that makes the planet turn as a planet, and it runs
	// on DriftTime, which never resets.
	// Boundary is 1 at a band transition, 0 in a plateau. Computed at the
	// UNWARPED latitude: it modulates the warp, so deriving it from the warped
	// coordinate would make the warp depend on itself.
    float JetShape, Boundary, JetRaw;
    GG_JetShaped(S, BandCount, Asymmetry, BandShape, JetShape, Boundary, JetRaw);

    const float Rate = GG_ZonalRate(S, BandCount, JetStrength, EquatorialBoost, Asymmetry, BandShape);

	// Turbulence follows the shear, and after plateau shaping the shear lives at
	// the boundaries. Zone interiors go nearly laminar, which is what stops the
	// bands reading as one scalloped curve repeated all the way round: the
	// scalloping now happens only where two jets are actually rubbing together.
	//
	// Warps.w is the floor, since a real zone interior is calmer than its edges
	// but not glass.
    const float Turbulence = lerp(Warps.w, 1.0f, Boundary);

	// ZERO MEAN by construction, which is what lets ShearFalloff differ per
	// layer without moving the layers apart.
	//
	// Rate - RigidRate was not zero-mean unless RigidRate happened to equal the
	// profile's mean, and a constant inside this term is a RIGID rotation:
	// scaling it by a per-layer falloff slides each layer to a different
	// longitude while changing nobody's strain. At the previous defaults that
	// left the coarse and detail layers most of a turn apart, sampling
	// unrelated parts of their own fields.
	//
	// The raw jet shape is symmetric about zero already, so only the part that
	// actually varies with latitude -- the part that actually winds anything --
	// gets scaled.
    const float Differential = JetStrength * JetRaw;

	// Transport, shared by every layer and never reset. Constant in S on
	// purpose: a latitude-varying transport term would wind without bound and
	// without a crossfade to catch it.
    const float ThetaRigid = RigidRate * DriftTime;

	// -- Coarse: full shear, big features, becomes the filaments -------------

    const float3 PCoarse = GG_RotateZ(P, -(ThetaRigid + Differential * ShearTime * ShearFalloff.x));

	// Integrated over GG_WARP_ITERATIONS steps rather than applied in one.
	//
	// Each step takes 1/N of the total displacement and re-samples where it
	// arrived, so the path follows the field instead of leaving along a single
	// tangent. Total distance travelled is unchanged; what changes is that the
	// result carries the streamline's CURVATURE. That is the entire difference
	// between a filament running straight off a band forever and one winding
	// back into a swirl, and with one step it is unobtainable no matter what
	// the field looks like -- a single displacement moves material once and
	// stops, so the shape of the streamline never appears in the output.
	//
	// Drift is separate from shear: shear moves material along the flow, drift
	// changes the flow itself. Without it the field is a fixed pattern stirred
	// forever with nothing new ever appearing.

	// Rate at the shading point's own latitude. The integration runs in the
	// frame CO-MOVING with this, which is what makes closed streamlines
	// possible at all: cat's eyes exist only in the frame moving with the
	// vortex, and in any other frame the same flow is open and streaming.
    const float RateHere = GG_ZonalRate(S, BandCount, JetStrength, EquatorialBoost, Asymmetry, BandShape);

    float3 Pos = PCoarse;

	// Warps.x is a TIME, not a displacement.
	//
	// A cat's eye needs roughly one vortex turnover to form. Treating the warp
	// as a fixed displacement caps how far the path can wind regardless of how
	// strong the flow is, so the roll-up could never complete and every
	// stretched contour ended in a rounded turnaround.
    const float StepTime = (Warps.x * Turbulence) / (float) GG_WARP_ITERATIONS;

    float Pressure = 0.0f;

	[unroll]
    for (int It = 0; It < GG_WARP_ITERATIONS; It++)
    {
        const float3 CoarseUVW = Pos * Scales.x + Drift * DriftTime;
        const float4 Coarse = CoarseFlow.SampleLevel(CoarseSampler, CoarseUVW, 0.0f);

        if (It == 0)
        {
			// From the unwarped position, so the pressure reported matches the
			// circulation that drove the first step. RGBA16F decodes identity;
			// a BGRA8 bake would need DecodeScale/DecodeBias here.
            Pressure = Coarse.a - 0.5f;
        }

		// SPIRAL. cross(N, grad psi) runs along the contours of psi, so its
		// streamlines circle a vortex and never approach or leave it. A
		// component ALONG the gradient crosses contours instead, so the path
		// spirals inward or outward by sign. Only meaningful because of the
		// loop -- the spiral is a property of the path, and one step has no
		// path. The tangential projection keeps it on the sphere; cross() got
		// that for free, this does not.
        const float3 GradTangent = Coarse.rgb - N * dot(N, Coarse.rgb);

		// Swirl.y converts the baked gradient into an ANGULAR RATE.
		//
		// This is the unit fix. Decoded grad psi is normalized to fill [-1,1]
		// and carries no physical scale whatever, while the jet profile is in
		// radians per unit time. Nothing made those two commensurate, so the
		// zonal term was being swamped by a factor of order a hundred and had
		// to be compensated by hand. With this, both terms below are rates and
		// their balance means something: cat's eyes form when the vortex turns
		// at about the rate the shear strains it, so Swirl.y wants to sit near
		// JetStrength.
        const float3 EddyVelocity = (cross(N, Coarse.rgb) + GradTangent * Swirl.x) * Swirl.y;

		// ZONAL SHEAR, exact rather than linearized.
		//
		// The previous form used du/dS times the latitude excursion, which is
		// zero on the first iteration by construction and a fraction of one
		// step on the second. The difference of the rates is the same quantity
		// without the small-excursion assumption, costs the same, and is valid
		// however far the path wanders.
		//
		// This is what puts the vortex INSIDE the shear layer. Simple shear
		// alone has no closed streamlines and can only elongate a blob, which
		// is why a stretched contour always ended in a rounded U -- that shape
		// was structural, not an artifact. A vortex plus shear closes the
		// streamlines into cat's eyes, and that is the billow shape a real
		// filament makes when it turns back.
		//
		// cross(Z, Pos) points east and already carries cos(latitude), so it
		// needs no normalizing and stays finite at the poles.
        const float RateThere = GG_ZonalRate(Pos.z, BandCount, JetStrength, EquatorialBoost, Asymmetry, BandShape);

        const float3 ZonalVelocity = cross(float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f), Pos) * (RateThere - RateHere);

		// Renormalized every step because the displacement leaves the sphere.
		// Only .z is read downstream, so a chord move would bias latitude, and
		// over several steps it compounds.
        Pos = normalize(Pos + (EddyVelocity + ZonalVelocity) * StepTime);
    }

    float3 Warped = Pos;

	// Net displacement as a difference rather than a running sum: each step
	// renormalizes, so the sum of the steps is not the distance travelled.
    const float3 CoarseWarp = Warped - PCoarse;

    float Ridge = 0.5f;
    float3 DetailWarp = 0.0f;

#if GG_DETAIL_OCTAVE

	// -- Detail: reduced shear, inherits the coarse displacement -------------

    float3 PDetail = GG_RotateZ(P, -(ThetaRigid + Differential * ShearTime * ShearFalloff.y));

	// Inherits only a FRACTION of the coarse displacement, and this is the term
	// that decides whether ShearFalloff.y appears to do anything at all.
	//
	// A displacement field with a large gradient IS strain, whatever it is
	// called. Once the coarse layer is wound, its features are narrower by the
	// shear factor and so grad(CoarseWarp) is large by exactly that factor.
	// Inheriting it whole imposes an order-one strain on the fine layer no
	// matter how little that layer was rotated, which buries its own falloff
	// completely.
	//
	// Physically the fraction is right too. A small eddy is regenerated before
	// it accumulates much strain, so it should be advected by a recent slice of
	// the flow's history rather than the whole of it, and the accumulated warp
	// is the whole of it.
    PDetail = normalize(PDetail + CoarseWarp * ShearFalloff.w);

	// Incommensurate drift rate so the layers never fall into a repeating beat.
    const float3 DetailUVW = PDetail * Scales.y + Drift * (DriftTime * 2.3f);
    const float4 DetailSample = DetailFlow.SampleLevel(DetailSampler, DetailUVW, 0.0f);

    DetailWarp = cross(N, DetailSample.rgb) * (Warps.y * Turbulence);
    Warped = normalize(Warped + DetailWarp);

	// The independent alpha. Worley F2-F1 is MINIMAL on cell walls, so the
	// filament network arrives dark and Ridge usually wants to be negative to
	// make it read as bright wisps.
    Ridge = DetailSample.a;
#endif

    float4 Packed = 0.5f;

#if GG_PACKED_DETAIL

	// -- Packed: least shear, rides both warps above -------------------------
	//
	// Sampled at a position carrying BOTH accumulated displacements, which is
	// what makes this ride the flow rather than float over it as an unrelated
	// layer. Its own shear is near zero, so it transports and deforms gently
	// without ever winding tight enough to alias.

    float3 PPacked = GG_RotateZ(P, -(ThetaRigid + Differential * ShearTime * ShearFalloff.z));
	// Its OWN inheritance, separate from the detail layer's, and 0 is a
	// legitimate setting rather than a degenerate one.
	//
	// This layer's job is to break the flow up into rounded organic shapes, and
	// a shape that has been dragged through the flow field is no longer round.
	// The other layers want to follow the flow because they ARE the flow; this
	// one is chemistry riding on top of it, and reads better held rigid than
	// stretched along with everything else.
    PPacked = normalize(PPacked + (CoarseWarp + DetailWarp) * Scales.w);

    const float3 PackedUVW = PPacked * Scales.z + Drift * (DriftTime * 0.7f);
    Packed = PackedDetail.SampleLevel(PackedSampler, PackedUVW, 0.0f);
#endif

	// Zonal flow cannot disturb a latitude-only pattern, so all band meander
	// comes from the warps above and this jitter -- never from JetStrength.
    const float BandCoord = Warped.z + Warps.z * Pressure;

    const float Band = GG_BandProfile(BandCoord, BandCount, Asymmetry, BandShape, BandSharpness);

	// EDGE WEIGHTING, which is the other half of why detail read as grain.
	//
	// 4*B*(1-B) peaks at the half-way point of a band transition and falls to
	// zero in a saturated zone or belt. Detail added uniformly is by definition
	// grain: it has no relationship to the structure it sits on. Detail
	// concentrated on the transitions ravels the band EDGES, which is where a
	// real gas giant's billows and curls live and where this construction
	// otherwise looks most obviously analytic.
	//
	// EdgeBias is how much survives in the flat interiors -- zero everywhere
	// but the edges is its own kind of artificial.
	// RELIEF THINNING.
	//
	// Shear stretches a parcel horizontally without adding material to it, so
	// an incompressible parcel stretched by g in one direction and unchanged in
	// the other must thin VERTICALLY by 1/g. Heavily wound regions therefore
	// belong lower and flatter than unwound ones, and this is what the fluid
	// would actually do rather than a correction applied to make it behave.
	//
	// Without it, filament width shrinks with winding while filament height
	// does not, so the aspect ratio runs away and the march reads them as
	// spikes -- fine at distance, sharp acute peaks up close. This is the term
	// that keeps width and height falling together.
	//
	// Analytic and per-LATITUDE, not per-filament. That is a real limitation,
	// but the winding here is driven by zonal shear and zonal shear is a
	// function of latitude, so it captures nearly all of the variation.
	//
	// Normalized by the band frequency so the parameter stays O(1) rather than
	// tracking whatever JetStrength and SpinUpTime happen to be: Winding reads
	// as turns per band rather than radians per unit S.
    const float Slope = GG_JetRawSlope(S, BandCount, Asymmetry);

    const float Winding = abs(JetStrength * Slope * ShearTime * ShearFalloff.x)
		/ max(BandCount * PI, 1e-3f);

    const float ReliefScale = 1.0f / (1.0f + BandShape.z * Winding);

    const float Edge = lerp(Detail.w, 1.0f, 4.0f * Band * (1.0f - Band));

	// R against G as Perlin-Worley: the fluffy cloud-shape term. B stays raw as
	// streaks, which want to look wispy rather than rounded.
    const float Fluff = GG_PerlinWorley(Packed.r, Packed.g);

    const float Modulation =
		  Detail.x * (Ridge - 0.5f)
		+ Detail.y * (Fluff - 0.5f)
		+ Detail.z * (Packed.b - 0.5f);

	// Pulled toward the neutral half rather than scaled, because Top is built
	// as TopBase + BandRelief * (Band - 0.5). Collapsing toward 0.5 is exactly
	// a reduction in relief, and it costs one lerp instead of another parameter
	// threaded down to GG_CloudTop.
    const float Composite = saturate(Band + Edge * Modulation);

    return float4(
		lerp(0.5f, Composite, ReliefScale),
		Pressure,
		Packed.a,
		Packed.g);
}

/** Full evaluation. Six fetches: two phases of three.
 *
 *  The packed layer is inside the crossfade now. It has to be, because it is
 *  sampled at a position that carries both warps, and those are per-phase. That
 *  is also what fixed it reading as an unrelated grain overlay.
 *
 *  Returns:
 *    x  Band brightness, composited. Feed through a colour ramp.
 *    y  Pressure. Negative is a low, so cyclonic; extrema are vortex centres
 *       and the circulation already matches them. Use it to tint spots.
 *    z  Composition, large scale. For hue variation across the disc.
 *    w  Storm cell mask. Discrete cells, for picking out individual features.
 *
 *  Scales        float4(Coarse, Detail, Packed, PackedWarpInherit)
 *  Warps         float4(CoarseWarp, DetailWarp, BandJitter, TurbulenceFloor)
 *  Detail        float4(Ridge, Mottle, Wisp, EdgeBias)
 *  ShearFalloff  float4(Coarse, Detail, Packed, DetailWarpInherit) */
float4 GG_GasGiant(
	Texture3D CoarseFlow,
	SamplerState CoarseSampler,
	Texture3D DetailFlow,
	SamplerState DetailSampler,
	Texture3D PackedDetail,
	SamplerState PackedSampler,
	float3 LocalPos,
	float PlanetRadius,
	float Time,
	float SpinUpTime,
	float CrossfadePeriod,
	float PhaseJitterAmount,
	float RigidRate,
	float3 Drift,
	float4 Scales,
	float4 Warps,
	float4 Detail,
	float4 ShearFalloff,
	float BandCount,
	float JetStrength,
	float EquatorialBoost,
	float Asymmetry,
	float3 BandShape,
	float BandSharpness,
	float2 Swirl)
{
    const float3 P = LocalPos / max(PlanetRadius, 1e-4f);
    const float3 N = normalize(P);
    const float S = N.z;

	// Static per-point offset, so regions sit at different points in their own
	// winding cycle and the reset never happens planet-wide at once.
    const float Jitter = GG_PhaseJitter(N) * PhaseJitterAmount;

    const float Period = max(CrossfadePeriod, 1e-3f);
    const float Cycle = Time / Period + Jitter;

    const float Phase0 = frac(Cycle);
    const float Phase1 = frac(Cycle + 0.5f);

	// Weight of phase ONE. It peaks at 1 when Phase0 is at 0 or 1, exactly
	// where phase 0 snaps from fully wound back to unwound, so phase 0
	// contributes nothing there.
    float Blend = abs(1.0f - 2.0f * Phase0);

	// Smoothstep for C1. The raw triangle is continuous but its derivative
	// flips sign instantly at the peak, and the eye reads that kink as a pop
	// even when the values either side match.
    Blend = Blend * Blend * (3.0f - 2.0f * Blend);

	// SpinUpTime dominates both, which is what makes the two phases nearly
	// identical in filament density and the reset nearly invisible.
    float Shear0 = SpinUpTime + Phase0 * Period;
    float Shear1 = SpinUpTime + Phase1 * Period;

#if GG_SINGLE_PHASE
	// Both phases identical, so the blend is a no-op and whatever remains is
	// the field rather than the crossfade.
	Shear1 = Shear0;
#endif

    const float4 Phase0Result = GG_EvaluatePhase(
		CoarseFlow, CoarseSampler, DetailFlow, DetailSampler, PackedDetail, PackedSampler,
		P, N, S, Shear0, Time, RigidRate, Drift, Scales, Warps, Detail, ShearFalloff,
		BandCount, JetStrength, EquatorialBoost, Asymmetry, BandShape, BandSharpness, Swirl);

    const float4 Phase1Result = GG_EvaluatePhase(
		CoarseFlow, CoarseSampler, DetailFlow, DetailSampler, PackedDetail, PackedSampler,
		P, N, S, Shear1, Time, RigidRate, Drift, Scales, Warps, Detail, ShearFalloff,
		BandCount, JetStrength, EquatorialBoost, Asymmetry, BandShape, BandSharpness, Swirl);

    float4 Result = lerp(Phase0Result, Phase1Result, Blend);

#if GG_DEBUG_CROSSFADE
	Result.x = Blend;
#endif

    return Result;
}

/** An authored vortex, added AFTER the shear rather than baked into the field
 *  that gets sheared.
 *
 *  This is not a convenience. Kinematic shear has no mechanism to sustain a
 *  vortex, so anything living inside the sheared field is eventually stretched
 *  into a filament and lost. Real spots survive because the inverse cascade
 *  feeds them continuously, and nothing here reproduces that. Exempting them
 *  from the shear is the honest fake, and it gives the right look besides: the
 *  spot persists while everything around it filaments past.
 *
 *  Centre is a unit direction. Drift its longitude by rotating Centre with
 *  GG_RotateZ at the spot's own band rate before calling. */
float GG_Spot(float3 N, float3 Centre, float Radius, float Strength)
{
    const float D = distance(N, normalize(Centre));

    return Strength * exp(-(D * D) / max(Radius * Radius, 1e-6f));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Density
//
// COVERAGE IS 100% AND THAT SIMPLIFIES EVERYTHING.
//
// Terrestrial cloud shaders spend most of their complexity on coverage: where
// is there cloud and where is there sky. A gas giant has no holes. There is no
// threshold here, no coverage remap, no cloud/no-cloud decision. Density is
// solid below the deck and falls off above it, and every visible feature is
// RELIEF in that top surface rather than variation in opacity.
//
// WHICH IS WHY BAND DRIVES ALTITUDE, NOT OPACITY.
//
// Zones and belts are not bright and dark patches of the same material. They
// are high and low cloud decks: a zone is a raised deck of fresh ammonia ice,
// a belt is where the deck sits low and the line of sight reaches warmer,
// darker material underneath. Brightness is the consequence. Feeding Band into
// density would be modelling the symptom and would look flat under any lighting
// model, because there would be no actual geometry for light to interact with.
//
// The useful side effect is that all the detail work -- ridges, mottling,
// wisps, and the edge weighting that concentrates them on transitions -- lands
// in the height field. Under a march it becomes surface relief along the cliffs
// between decks, which is where a real gas giant's texture lives.
//
// COMPOSITION IS NOT A DENSITY TERM.
//
// Packed.a is a chemistry marker. It says what the material IS, not how much
// of it there is, so it belongs in extinction or scattering colour, not in the
// density field. It is passed straight through below rather than composited.
// Folding it in would make chemically distinct regions physically thinner,
// which is wrong and, worse, would look almost right.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Cloud top altitude, in fractions of the shell thickness.
 *
 *  Relief is float4(TopBase, BandRelief, PressureLift, StormTowers).
 *
 *  The pressure sign is not arbitrary. Anticyclones are high pressure, rising,
 *  and condense fresh ammonia above the haze -- they are the bright ovals and
 *  the Great Red Spot, and they must sit HIGH. Cyclones sink and expose deeper
 *  material, so they must sit low. Getting this backwards produces a field that
 *  reads as plausible until you notice every storm is a pit. */
float GG_CloudTop(float4 Field, float4 Relief, float VortexThreshold)
{
    const float Band = Field.x;
    const float Pressure = Field.y;
    const float StormCells = Field.w;

    float Top = Relief.x + Relief.y * (Band - 0.5f);

    Top += Relief.z * Pressure;

	// Convective towers, only where the circulation supports one. Ungated,
	// Worley F1 is polka dots; gated on vortex strength it becomes storms that
	// sit where the flow says they should. Inverted because F1 is minimal at
	// cell CENTRES, which is where the tower belongs.
    const float Vortex = smoothstep(VortexThreshold, 0.5f, abs(Pressure));
    Top += Relief.w * (1.0f - StormCells) * Vortex;

    return Top;
}

/** Density at a point in the shell.
 *
 *  LocalPos here carries its true radius, unlike everywhere else in this file.
 *  The columnar field is evaluated at the normalized direction: the shell is
 *  thin enough that sampling at the true radius would shift UVW by far less
 *  than one lattice cell, so it would cost the same and change nothing.
 *
 *  Profile is float4(ShellThickness, TopSoftness, VortexThreshold, CoreRadius).
 *
 *  Returns:
 *    x  Density in [0,1]. Multiply by an extinction coefficient in the march.
 *    y  Composition, for scattering colour. NOT a density term.
 *    z  Pressure, for tinting vortices.
 *    w  Cloud top altitude, for debugging and for sphere-tracing the surface.
 *
 *  Note the march wants BandSharpness low -- around 0.8. High sharpness
 *  saturates Band toward 0 and 1, which turns the height field into two flat
 *  terraces joined by a cliff and throws away the relief this exists to
 *  produce. Sharpness was doing the work of contrast back when the output was
 *  brightness; here geometry does it instead. */
float4 GG_Density(
	Texture3D CoarseFlow,
	SamplerState CoarseSampler,
	Texture3D DetailFlow,
	SamplerState DetailSampler,
	Texture3D PackedDetail,
	SamplerState PackedSampler,
	float3 LocalPos,
	float PlanetRadius,
	float Time,
	float SpinUpTime,
	float CrossfadePeriod,
	float PhaseJitterAmount,
	float RigidRate,
	float3 Drift,
	float4 Scales,
	float4 Warps,
	float4 Detail,
	float4 ShearFalloff,
	float BandCount,
	float JetStrength,
	float EquatorialBoost,
	float Asymmetry,
	float3 BandShape,
	float BandSharpness,
	float2 Swirl,
	float4 Relief,
	float4 Profile)
{
    const float Radius = length(LocalPos);
    const float3 Direction = LocalPos / max(Radius, 1e-4f);

	// Shell fraction. 0 at the deck base, 1 at the top of the modelled shell.
    const float Height = (Radius - PlanetRadius) / max(Profile.x, 1e-4f);

    const float4 Field = GG_GasGiant(
		CoarseFlow, CoarseSampler, DetailFlow, DetailSampler, PackedDetail, PackedSampler,
		Direction * PlanetRadius, PlanetRadius,
		Time, SpinUpTime, CrossfadePeriod, PhaseJitterAmount, RigidRate,
		Drift, Scales, Warps, Detail, ShearFalloff,
		BandCount, JetStrength, EquatorialBoost, Asymmetry, BandShape, BandSharpness, Swirl);

    const float Top = GG_CloudTop(Field, Relief, Profile.z);

	// Solid below the deck, fading above it. No lower bound is applied: the
	// interior is opaque and never seen, and fading density toward the base
	// would carve a hollow that only shows up as a wrong silhouette at the limb.
	//
	// Smoothstep rather than a linear ramp. The linear form is C0 at both ends,
	// and those two kinks are visible as hard lines at the cloud top and at the
	// point the deck goes solid -- the top one especially, since it is the
	// silhouette. Softening the curve buys more apparent softness than raising
	// TopSoftness does, and it costs nothing.
    const float Softness = max(Profile.y, 1e-4f);
    const float Density = 1.0f - smoothstep(Top - Softness, Top, Height);

    return float4(Density, Field.z, Field.y, Top);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Raymarch
//
// A SHELL, NOT A BOX.
//
// NoiseVolumePreview marches a box, and the note at the top of that file
// explains why: a tiling volume's domain is a cube and the seams it exists to
// find sit on the faces. Neither reason survives here. This field is not being
// validated for tiling, it is a spherical atmosphere, and the same file records
// that the galaxy marcher it descended from used a sphere for exactly this
// reason. So this goes back to a sphere, and specifically to a thin SHELL.
//
// THE SPAN IS ANALYTICALLY THIN, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS AFFORDABLE.
//
// Each density sample here costs six texture fetches, against one for the box
// marcher. Stepping the whole atmosphere at that price would be hopeless.
//
// It is not necessary. GG_CloudTop is a bounded expression: Band and Pressure
// are both bounded, and the storm term is one-sided, so the highest and lowest
// the deck can possibly reach are known in closed form from Relief alone. Above
// TopMax there is provably nothing. Below TopMin minus the softness width,
// density is provably saturated and the view can never reach further.
//
// So the only span worth marching is the thin band between those two radii,
// and everything outside it is skipped without a single sample. This is the
// same argument the baker's brick bounds make -- bound the field, then use the
// bound to avoid sampling it -- applied to an analytic term rather than a baked
// one, which makes it exact rather than conservative.
//
// The consequence is that step budget buys resolution of the CLOUD TOP rather
// than of the atmosphere, which is the only thing visible at 100% coverage.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Interleaved gradient noise, from pixel coordinates.
 *
 *  Computed here rather than fed in, because the only thing a caller could
 *  usefully supply is the pixel position, and it is already in scope at the
 *  Custom node as Parameters.SvPosition. A separate node to produce this was a
 *  node whose entire job was to be forgotten and left at a constant, which
 *  silently disables the jitter and looks exactly like a marcher with too few
 *  steps.
 *
 *  IGN rather than a hash: its output is decorrelated between neighbouring
 *  pixels but low-discrepancy over any small block, so the error averages out
 *  across a few pixels instead of turning into salt-and-pepper. That matters
 *  because the thing being hidden is a sampling lattice, which is itself a
 *  block-scale artifact.
 *
 *  Spatial only. Adding View.StateFrameIndexMod8 to the dot product gives a
 *  temporally varying version, which is better under TAA and reads as flicker
 *  without it. */
float GG_Jitter(float2 PixelPos)
{
    return frac(52.9829189f * frac(dot(PixelPos, float2(0.06711056f, 0.00583715f))));
}

/** Ray against a sphere centred on the origin. Direction must be normalized.
 *
 *  Returns false when the ray misses. T0 may be negative, which means the
 *  origin is inside; callers clamp rather than reject, since a camera inside
 *  the shell is a normal case and not an error. */
bool GG_RaySphere(float3 Origin, float3 Dir, float Radius, out float T0, out float T1)
{
    const float B = dot(Origin, Dir);
    const float C = dot(Origin, Origin) - Radius * Radius;

    const float Disc = B * B - C;

    if (Disc < 0.0f)
    {
        T0 = 0.0f;
        T1 = 0.0f;
        return false;
    }

    const float S = sqrt(Disc);

    T0 = -B - S;
    T1 = -B + S;

    return true;
}

/** Highest and lowest the deck can reach, in shell fractions.
 *
 *  Closed form, not a probe. Band lands in [0,1] so its centred form is bounded
 *  by half its amplitude; Pressure is a normalized channel minus 0.5 and is
 *  bounded the same way; the storm term is one-sided because both its gate and
 *  its mask are non-negative.
 *
 *  Deliberately exact rather than padded. A loose bound here costs steps on
 *  guaranteed-empty space, which is precisely what this exists to avoid. */
void GG_TopBounds(float4 Relief, out float OutMin, out float OutMax)
{
    const float BandSpan = 0.5f * abs(Relief.y);
    const float PressureSpan = 0.5f * abs(Relief.z);

    OutMin = Relief.x - BandSpan - PressureSpan;
    OutMax = Relief.x + BandSpan + PressureSpan + max(Relief.w, 0.0f);
}

/** Marches the cloud shell and integrates density.
 *
 *  InViewVec       Local camera vector, surface toward camera. CameraVector
 *                  through TransformVector [World -> Local].
 *  InCamPosLocal   Camera position in PLANET-LOCAL space, in the same units as
 *                  PlanetRadius. This is the ray origin, so a wrong value moves
 *                  the ray rather than merely biasing step size.
 *
 *  InDensityScale  Extinction multiplier. Because coverage is total, this wants
 *                  to be high enough that the march saturates within a few
 *                  steps of the deck; a low value produces a translucent planet
 *                  that reads as fog rather than as cloud.
 *
 *  InStepBudget    A budget, not a count. See the note in NoiseVolumePreview.
 *  InStepGrowth    Step growth across the marched span.
 *  InMaxSteps      Safety bound. Set to int(StepBudget) + 8.
 *  InPixelPos      Parameters.SvPosition.xy at the Custom node.
 *  InJitterAmount  0 disables the jitter, 1 applies it fully. Anything in
 *                  between mixes toward no offset.
 *
 *  Returns float4:
 *    x  Cloud top altitude at the visible surface. THIS is the image to look
 *       at. Note what is NOT returned: accumulated emission. For a uniformly
 *       emitting medium the emission integral is identically 1 - Transmittance,
 *       so it carries no structure whatever and is recoverable from y anyway.
 *    y  Transmittance. 1 - this is opacity.
 *    z  Composition, for scattering colour.
 *    w  Pressure, for tinting vortices.
 *
 *  z and w are integrated against the same weights as the density itself, so
 *  each is the expected value over what the ray actually sees rather than a
 *  reading from an arbitrary step. That is what makes them usable as shading
 *  inputs: at the limb, where a ray grazes many columns, a single-step reading
 *  would flicker.
 *
 *  Packed into one float4 rather than returned through out parameters because
 *  the Custom node's Additional Outputs have to be declared by hand in the
 *  node's own array, and a mismatch there fails at shader compile with an
 *  undeclared identifier rather than anywhere useful. Four channels is what a
 *  single return can carry, and these are the four that matter. */
float4 GG_March(
	Texture3D CoarseFlow,
	SamplerState CoarseSampler,
	Texture3D DetailFlow,
	SamplerState DetailSampler,
	Texture3D PackedDetail,
	SamplerState PackedSampler,
	float3 InViewVec,
	float3 InCamPosLocal,
	float PlanetRadius,
	float Time,
	float SpinUpTime,
	float CrossfadePeriod,
	float PhaseJitterAmount,
	float RigidRate,
	float3 Drift,
	float4 Scales,
	float4 Warps,
	float4 Detail,
	float4 ShearFalloff,
	float BandCount,
	float JetStrength,
	float EquatorialBoost,
	float Asymmetry,
	float3 BandShape,
	float BandSharpness,
	float2 Swirl,
	float4 Relief,
	float4 Profile,
	float InDensityScale,
	float InStepBudget,
	float InStepGrowth,
	int InMaxSteps,
	float2 InPixelPos,
	float InJitterAmount)
{
	// Miss value. Transmittance 1 means nothing was hit, which is what every
	// early exit below wants to report.
    const float4 Miss = float4(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);

    const float3 Origin = InCamPosLocal;
    const float3 Dir = normalize(-InViewVec);

    const float Thickness = max(Profile.x, 1e-4f);
    const float Softness = max(Profile.y, 1e-4f);

	// -- Shell bounds -------------------------------------------------------

    float TopMin, TopMax;
    GG_TopBounds(Relief, TopMin, TopMax);

	// No margin above TopMax. Density is saturate((Top - Height)/Softness),
	// which is exactly zero at Height == Top, so there is provably nothing
	// there and padding it would only buy empty steps.
    const float OuterRadius = PlanetRadius + Thickness * TopMax;

	// Below this the field is saturated everywhere, so the ray can never see
	// past it. It is the backstop, not a surface.
	//
	// Profile.w overrides it when positive, which is what you want once a solid
	// core mesh is drawn inside: shrink this to sit just outside that mesh and
	// the atmosphere stops where the surface begins, instead of at whatever
	// radius the deck bound happened to imply.
    const float DerivedInner = PlanetRadius + Thickness * max(TopMin - Softness, 0.0f);
    const float InnerRadius = (Profile.w > 0.0f) ? Profile.w : DerivedInner;

    float TEnter, TExit;
    if (!GG_RaySphere(Origin, Dir, OuterRadius, TEnter, TExit))
    {
        return Miss;
    }

    if (TExit <= 0.0f)
    {
        return Miss;
    }

	// Camera inside the shell falls out of this clamp rather than needing its
	// own branch, and it matters under a span-relative step rule: the stretch
	// behind the viewer would otherwise be budgeted as if visible, spending
	// steps on nothing AND coarsening everything that is not.
    float TStart = max(TEnter, 0.0f);

	// Clip against the opaque interior. Anything past the near intersection is
	// fully dense by construction, so marching it would burn steps to confirm
	// what the bound already guarantees.
	//
	// Hitting it must also terminate the ray OPAQUE. Clipping the span without
	// zeroing transmittance is what produced a hard ring where grazing rays ran
	// out of march before they ran out of medium -- the geometry stopped but the
	// opacity did not, so the shell appeared to thin out against a boundary that
	// is physically solid. Anything at or below this radius is saturated by
	// construction, so the ray genuinely cannot see past it.
    bool bHitCore = false;

    float TInner0, TInner1;
    if (GG_RaySphere(Origin, Dir, InnerRadius, TInner0, TInner1))
    {
        if (TInner0 > TStart)
        {
            TExit = min(TExit, TInner0);
            bHitCore = true;
        }
    }

    if (TExit <= TStart)
    {
        return Miss;
    }

	// -- Step sizing --------------------------------------------------------

    const float Span = max(TExit - TStart, 1e-6f);
    const float BaseStep = Span / max(InStepBudget, 1.0f);

	// Step floor from the field rather than an authored distance. The finest
	// structure along the ray is the deck's own transition width, since nothing
	// resolves more finely than the softness over which density goes from zero
	// to one. Half of that is the Nyquist limit and stepping below it resolves
	// detail the field does not contain.
	//
	// This is the vertical limit. Horizontal detail is set by the texture
	// scales and matters only at the limb, where the ray runs nearly tangent
	// and crosses many columns per step -- which is also where step growth
	// hurts most, so keep growth modest if the terminator looks banded.
    const float MinStep = max(Thickness * Softness * 0.5f, 1e-5f);

    const float Growth = max(InStepGrowth, 0.0f);
    const float InvSpan = 1.0f / Span;

	// Shortens the first step only, offsetting each pixel's lattice so the
	// sampling grid does not read as banding. Costs no energy: the final step
	// clamps to the exit, so total path length is unchanged.
	//
	// Lerped from 1 rather than used directly, so an amount of 0 means no
	// offset at all rather than a zero-length first step.
    const float Noise = GG_Jitter(InPixelPos);
    const float FirstScale = clamp(lerp(1.0f, Noise, saturate(InJitterAmount)), 0.05f, 1.0f);

	// -- March --------------------------------------------------------------

    float Transmittance = 1.0f;
    float Emission = 0.0f;
    float Composition = 0.0f;
    float Pressure = 0.0f;
    float TopAccum = 0.0f;

    float T = TStart;
    float StepsTaken = 0.0f;

	[loop]
    for (int i = 0; i < InMaxSteps; i++)
    {
        const float Remaining = TExit - T;
        if (Remaining <= 1e-6f)
        {
            break;
        }

		// Growth runs on progress along the span, not distance from the camera,
		// which keeps the step count independent of where the viewer stands.
        const float T01 = saturate((T - TStart) * InvSpan);

        float StepLen = max(BaseStep * (1.0f + Growth * T01), MinStep);
        if (i == 0)
        {
            StepLen *= FirstScale;
        }
        StepLen = min(StepLen, Remaining);

        StepsTaken += 1.0f;

		// Midpoint sample. With steps that grow, sampling at the near edge
		// weights every interval by its own leading face and biases the
		// integral toward the camera.
        const float3 SamplePos = Origin + Dir * (T + StepLen * 0.5f);

        const float4 Field = GG_Density(
			CoarseFlow, CoarseSampler, DetailFlow, DetailSampler, PackedDetail, PackedSampler,
			SamplePos, PlanetRadius,
			Time, SpinUpTime, CrossfadePeriod, PhaseJitterAmount, RigidRate,
			Drift, Scales, Warps, Detail, ShearFalloff,
			BandCount, JetStrength, EquatorialBoost, Asymmetry, BandShape, BandSharpness, Swirl,
			Relief, Profile);

        const float Alpha = 1.0f - exp(-Field.x * InDensityScale * StepLen);
        const float Weight = Alpha * Transmittance;

        Emission += Weight;
        Composition += Weight * Field.y;
        Pressure += Weight * Field.z;
        TopAccum += Weight * Field.w;

        Transmittance *= 1.0f - Alpha;

        if (Transmittance < 0.001f)
        {
            break;
        }

        T += StepLen;
    }

	// Normalize the integrated properties by the weight that produced them, so
	// they stay in their own units instead of scaling with opacity. Guarded
	// because a ray that grazed the shell without accumulating anything has no
	// composition to report and must not divide by zero.
	// The march stopped against the solid interior, so nothing gets through.
    if (bHitCore)
    {
        Emission += Transmittance;
        Transmittance = 0.0f;
    }

    const float TotalWeight = max(Emission, 1e-4f);

    float TopOut = TopAccum / TotalWeight;

#if GG_DEBUG_STEPS
	TopOut = StepsTaken / max((float)InMaxSteps, 1.0f);
#endif

    return float4(
		TopOut,
		Transmittance,
		Composition / TotalWeight,
		Pressure / TotalWeight);
}
